Hewitson et al. (2021) looked for a panel of serum proteins that would
seperate boys with autism specturm disorder(ASD) from typically developing
(TD) boys. They studied 154 boys, 76 with ASD and 78 TD aged 18 months to
8 years. Serum samples were run on the SomaLogic SOMAScan 1.3k platform
which measured 1,317 proteins. Out of those, 192 proteins weren't used 
because they failed quality control. So 1,125 proteins were used. Protein
levels were log_10 transformed and z-scored and any value beyond +3 or -3
standard deviations was removed.

The study used 3 seperate methods to determine the what proteins are 
impactful. They used a random forest to classify ASD and TD and was fit 
1,000 times. The proteins were ranked by their avg MeanDecreaseGini
importance. They also used t-tests. Each protein had 2 smaple t-test 
comparing ASD and TD, the 10 most significant were kept. The last method
they ysed was correlation with severity. Each protein was correlated with
the ADOS total score. Then the 10 largeest absolute correlation were kept.

They compared the top 10 proteins for all 3 methods and 5 proteins were
found to be in all 3. This core panel is made of MAPK14, IgD, DERM, 
EPHB2, and suPAR. They also added 4 other proteins that helped raise the
AUC. ROR1, GI24, eIF-4H, and ARSB. So the final panel had 9 proteins

Then the study evaluated the panel, so they fit a logistic regression on
a random 80% selection of the boys and scored it on the other 20%. They
repeated this 1,000 times and averaged the results. The avg AUC was 0.860
+- 0.064, avg sensitvity was 0.833 +- 0.118, and avg specificity was 0.846
+- 0.118.

There are 2 points of contention for this study. Number 1 was that the
paper used all the data to select proteins. Thus the test split of boys
was used to help choose the best panel of proteins. However that makes 
the performance look better than it would be on new children. Number 2
was that the correlation analysis included the TD boys. They had 
randomly assigned ADOS scores and that made the correlation results 
unreliable. Due to these issues the paper was retracted in 2024.


